;;; A line separator between two forms is read as a space —
;;; **W73**, regression `line-separator`.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The last line holds two facts, `(p j)` and `(p k)`, with U+2028, the line
;;; separator, between them: an editor may show a line break there, and Ein's
;;; positions count lines at `\n` alone.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A character that is not ASCII is written only inside a string literal,
;;; after `:why`, `:source` or `:goal-text`, or in a comment (Q-M1f.95; a
;;; comment, Q-M1f.65): anywhere else
;;; the program is refused at the lexer, at the character, by a line that
;;; names it and its code point and says where Unicode is allowed, and every
;;; command exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). A Unicode space is no exception: a
;;; separator is ASCII whitespace.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `724c11e8`, release: the program loads, the line separator a separator,
;;; both facts stored, R two states, exit 0 on every command.

(relation a Thing :why "{?1} is an a")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is a p")
(is-a k Thing)
(is-a j Thing)
(a k)
(p j) (p k)
